    "body": "Walk into a pub in Ennis on a Thursday night and you may hear three fiddles, a flute, a box accordion, and a bodhrán negotiating the turn from The Silver Spear into The Musical Priest without a word spoken. Walk into a hall in Mullingar in August and you may hear the same tune played by a fifteen-year-old who has practised it for months to meet adjudicators' standards. Both scenes are Irish traditional music. Both are \"trad.\" Neither is more authentic than the other — but they answer different social needs, and a musician who understands only one will miss half the picture. This lesson is your map. Before you study instruments, tune forms, dance, or the long history of collectors and 78 rpm records, you need orientation: what people mean by trad versus Celtic, where the music lives, what the main tune types feel like, how songs sit alongside dance music, and which institutions keep the tradition moving. Think of it as arriving in a new city with a good guidebook rather than a single street address. Irish traditional…\n\nWalk into a pub in Ennis on a Thursday night and you may hear three fiddles, a flute, a box accordion, and a bodhrán negotiating the turn from The Silver Spear into The Musical Priest without a word spoken. Walk into a hall in Mullingar in August and you may hear the same tune played by a fifteen-year-old who has practised it for months to meet adjudicators' standards. Both scenes are Irish traditional music. Both are \"trad.\" Neither is more authentic than the other — but they answer different social needs, and a musician who understands only one will miss half the picture. This lesson is your map. Before you study instruments, tune forms, dance, or the long history of…",
