    "estimateMinutes": 4,
    "body": "The Gaeltacht (Gaeltachtaí in plural) denotes regions where Irish remains a community language — parts of Connemara, the Aran Islands, Donegal's west, Kerry's Dingle peninsula, and other pockets. Traditional music and Gaeltacht life overlap but are not the same thing. Plenty of world-class trad musicians grew up in English-speaking Dublin, Belfast, or Chicago; plenty of Gaeltacht communities face the same global pop pressures as anywhere else. Still, the Gaeltacht matters for sean-nós song (amhránaíocht ar an sean-nós — \"old-style singing\"), where Irish-language poetry, local dialect, and personal phrasing survive in unaccompanied performance. Oral transmission means the tune passes person to person: at a kitchen table, a summer-school class, a pub session, a feis waiting room. The melody may shift — a extra turn in the B part, a different name, a new key to suit a whistle. That variation is a feature, not a bug. Print and recording freeze moments in time; the living tradition keeps moving. When Michael Coleman recorded The Boys of the Lough in New York in 1921, he fixed one version that became \"standard\" for generations…\n\nThe Gaeltacht (Gaeltachtaí in plural) denotes regions where Irish remains a community language — parts of Connemara, the Aran Islands, Donegal's west, Kerry's Dingle peninsula, and other pockets. Traditional music and Gaeltacht life overlap but are not the same thing. Plenty of world-class trad musicians grew up in English-speaking Dublin, Belfast, or Chicago; plenty of Gaeltacht communities face the same global pop pressures as anywhere else. Still, the Gaeltacht matters for sean-nós song (amhránaíocht ar an sean-nós — \"old-style singing\"), where Irish-language poetry, local dialect, and personal phrasing survive in unaccompanied performance. Oral transmission means the tune passes person to person: at a kitchen table, a summer-school class, a pub session, a feis waiting room. The melody may shift —…",
