    "estimateMinutes": 4,
    "body": "A session is an informal gathering where musicians play tunes in sets — usually two or three tunes of the same form before switching. The word seisiún as a label for these gatherings is relatively recent (Wikipedia notes it was \"invented in the 1990s\" in some Irish circles), but the practice is old: neighbours with fiddles and flutes playing for dancers and listeners. Sessions happen in pubs, festival tents, summer-school corridors, and private homes. They are social contracts. Unwritten rules vary by room, but the following will serve you almost anywhere: Listen before you join. Arrive early, buy a drink if you're in a pub, stand where you can hear, and count how many tunes make a set before anyone switches form. Jumping in mid-tune or mid-set disrupts the room. Regulars notice newcomers who respect the music; they also notice newcomers who treat the session as a backing track for their solo. Humility is not weakness — it is how you earn the nod to start the next set. Know when not to play. If the room is packed with…\n\nA session is an informal gathering where musicians play tunes in sets — usually two or three tunes of the same form before switching. The word seisiún as a label for these gatherings is relatively recent (Wikipedia notes it was \"invented in the 1990s\" in some Irish circles), but the practice is old: neighbours with fiddles and flutes playing for dancers and listeners. Sessions happen in pubs, festival tents, summer-school corridors, and private homes. They are social contracts. Unwritten rules vary by room, but the following will serve you almost anywhere: Listen before you join. Arrive early, buy a drink if you're in a pub, stand where you can hear, and count how many tunes make a set before anyone…",
