    "estimateMinutes": 4,
    "body": "You do not need medieval Irish to play a reel, but the tradition's depth is real. Irish annals name musicians from the early medieval period onward: harpers, tiompanists (players of the tiompan, a stringed instrument), singers, and instrument makers. Wikipedia's Music of Ireland lists entries such as 1226: Aed mac Donn Ó Sochlachain, skilled at tuning harps and making instruments; 1168: Amhlaeibh Mac Innaighneorach, chief ollamh of Ireland in harp-playing; and even an annalistic note from 921 BC naming Cú Congalta, a \"Tethra\" (singer or orator). Harpers were patronised by Gaelic aristocracy until the collapse of that world in the eighteenth century. Turlough Carolan (1670–1738) composed more than two hundred pieces for patrons, blending bardic and baroque taste. Edward Bunting notated surviving harpers at the Belfast Harp Festival of 1792 — a rescue mission at the edge of extinction. That long arc — from annals to Carolan to Chicago police chief Francis O'Neill's tune books to UNESCO piping — is the subject of Lesson 6: History, Revival, and Transmission. Hold this preview: Irish trad is not a 1950s invention; it…\n\nYou do not need medieval Irish to play a reel, but the tradition's depth is real. Irish annals name musicians from the early medieval period onward: harpers, tiompanists (players of the tiompan, a stringed instrument), singers, and instrument makers. Wikipedia's Music of Ireland lists entries such as 1226: Aed mac Donn Ó Sochlachain, skilled at tuning harps and making instruments; 1168: Amhlaeibh Mac Innaighneorach, chief ollamh of Ireland in harp-playing; and even an annalistic note from 921 BC naming Cú Congalta, a \"Tethra\" (singer or orator). Harpers were patronised by Gaelic aristocracy until the collapse of that world in the eighteenth century. Turlough Carolan (1670–1738) composed more than two hundred pieces for patrons, blending bardic and baroque taste. Edward Bunting…",
