    "body": "If you are picking up an instrument for this unit, begin with listening before technique drills. Build a playlist tagged by form (reel, jig, hornpipe) and region (Sligo, Clare, Donegal). Attend a local session as audience before you play. Learn one tune thoroughly — both parts, at session tempo, with basic ornament — rather than half-learning twenty. Find a teacher or mentor if possible; even short corrections in bow grip or whistle embouchure save years of habit. If you are a theorist or classroom teacher, use this overview to frame discussions of orality, cultural nationalism, and diaspora — topics Lesson 6 develops historically. Irish trad is a case study in how music survives colonisation, language shift, and emigration not as a sealed relic but as a negotiated living practice. The tradition asks for participation, not only observation. Whether you compete at a fleadh, sit in at a pub, or teach the next generation a polka in a community hall, you join a chain of transmission stretching from medieval harpers to Chicago 78s to tonight's session. The following lessons give you…\n\nIf you are picking up an instrument for this unit, begin with listening before technique drills. Build a playlist tagged by form (reel, jig, hornpipe) and region (Sligo, Clare, Donegal). Attend a local session as audience before you play. Learn one tune thoroughly — both parts, at session tempo, with basic ornament — rather than half-learning twenty. Find a teacher or mentor if possible; even short corrections in bow grip or whistle embouchure save years of habit. If you are a theorist or classroom teacher, use this overview to frame discussions of orality, cultural nationalism, and diaspora — topics Lesson 6 develops historically. Irish trad is a case study in how music survives colonisation, language shift, and emigration not as…",
