    "lessonSourceId": "regions-celtic-ireland-02-instruments-voices-i",
    "estimateMinutes": 4,
    "body": "This lesson examines five instruments at the heart of Irish traditional music: the fiddle, flute, tin whistle, uilleann pipes, and Celtic harp (cláirseach). Guidebooks and festival programmes often group them as \"melody instruments\" — a shorthand that helps beginners find the tune line in a noisy pub, but a misleading map if you treat it as law. A fiddle bow can drive rhythm like a drum; uilleann pipes play chordal regulators behind the chanter; a harpist may harmonise a song or step into the tune line in a small ensemble. Session culture rewards listening over labelling: the same player may lead one set, drop to accompaniment the next, and sit out a tune in a key they do not know. The five voices share a family resemblance — diatonic tune types in D, G, A, and related modes; ornament built from cuts, rolls, and articulation rather than romantic vibrato; repertoire learned by ear from recordings, classes, and nights in kitchens. They differ in breath versus bow, in indoor versus outdoor volume, and in regional accent. A Sligo fiddle roll is…\n\nThis lesson examines five instruments at the heart of Irish traditional music: the fiddle, flute, tin whistle, uilleann pipes, and Celtic harp (cláirseach). Guidebooks and festival programmes often group them as \"melody instruments\" — a shorthand that helps beginners find the tune line in a noisy pub, but a misleading map if you treat it as law. A fiddle bow can drive rhythm like a drum; uilleann pipes play chordal regulators behind the chanter; a harpist may harmonise a song or step into the tune line in a small ensemble. Session culture rewards listening over labelling: the same player may lead one set, drop to accompaniment the next, and sit out a tune in a key they do not know.…",
