    "body": "The Irish fiddle is the violin played with traditional bowing, ornamentation, and intonation habits. Richard Head's 1674 account of Sunday life in Ireland — \"in every field a fiddle, and the lasses footing until they foam up\" — shows how deeply the instrument was tied to dance before concert stages or recording studios existed. Dublin maker Thomas Perry (c. 1744–1818) and others mass-produced violins on English models, putting affordable instruments into rural hands. Emigration carried fiddlers to London, New York, and Chicago; 78 rpm records sent styles back across the Atlantic so that a Sligo accent heard in County Mayo might have been learned from a New York pressing.\n\nThe Irish fiddle is the violin played with traditional bowing, ornamentation, and intonation habits. Richard Head's 1674 account of Sunday life in Ireland — \"in every field a fiddle, and the lasses footing until they foam up\" — shows how deeply the instrument was tied to dance before concert stages or recording studios existed. Dublin maker Thomas Perry (c. 1744–1818) and others mass-produced violins on English models, putting affordable instruments into rural hands. Emigration carried fiddlers to London, New York, and Chicago; 78 rpm records sent styles back across the Atlantic so that a Sligo accent heard in County Mayo might have been learned from a New York pressing.",
