    "body": "Irish fiddlers typically hold the instrument in standard violin posture — chin on rest, left hand on the neck — but the sound and articulation diverge sharply from classical training. Vibrato is used sparingly, most often on slow airs; dance tunes rely on cuts (a quick grace note above the main pitch), rolls (ornament patterns around a sustained note), trebles (bowed triplets), and slides into target notes. Bowing carries regional identity: slurs into the beat on reels create lift; Donegal players use stronger detaché and bow pressure; Clare players may favour a slightly looser, loping pulse on polkas and slides.\n\nOpen strings ring as drone colour — especially G and D in fiddle-friendly keys. Tunings are standard G-D-A-E unless a player experiments with scordatura for a particular tune (uncommon but not unknown in older regional practice). Many players learn by ear at kitchen sessions; written scores from O'Neill's Music of Ireland or online ABC files are references, not substitutes for listening to phrasing.\n\nIrish fiddlers typically hold the instrument in standard violin posture — chin on rest, left hand on the neck — but the sound and articulation diverge sharply from classical training. Vibrato is used sparingly, most often on slow airs; dance tunes rely on cuts (a quick grace note above the main pitch), rolls (ornament patterns around a sustained note), trebles (bowed triplets), and slides into target notes. Bowing carries regional identity: slurs into the beat on reels create lift; Donegal players use stronger detaché and bow pressure; Clare players may favour a slightly looser, loping pulse on polkas and slides. Open strings ring as drone colour — especially G and D in fiddle-friendly keys. Tunings are standard G-D-A-E unless a…",
