    "body": "The Irish flute is usually a simple-system wooden flute — six tone holes and a conical bore — often pitched in D. It is not the Boehm-system silver flute of orchestras: trad players favour a breathy, reedy tone with flexible intonation and strong ornament in the same vocabulary as whistle and fiddle (cuts, rolls, slides, cran on low D). Nineteenth-century classical orchestras adopted metal Boehm flutes; obsolete wooden instruments flooded into rural Ireland at low cost, and players reclaimed them for dance music. Modern makers (Patrick Olwell, Hammy Hamilton, and others) build new simple-system flutes for trad demand.\n\nThe Irish flute is usually a simple-system wooden flute — six tone holes and a conical bore — often pitched in D. It is not the Boehm-system silver flute of orchestras: trad players favour a breathy, reedy tone with flexible intonation and strong ornament in the same vocabulary as whistle and fiddle (cuts, rolls, slides, cran on low D). Nineteenth-century classical orchestras adopted metal Boehm flutes; obsolete wooden instruments flooded into rural Ireland at low cost, and players reclaimed them for dance music. Modern makers (Patrick Olwell, Hammy Hamilton, and others) build new simple-system flutes for trad demand.",
