    "body": "This lesson covers instruments that entered Irish sessions across different centuries — bodhrán, bones, guitar, bouzouki, mandolin, tenor banjo, concertina, button accordion, and harmonica. Some are indigenous or long-rooted (frame drum, bones); others arrived through diaspora, jazz age, or 1960s–70s fusion (guitar, bouzouki, banjo in trad lineup). All are now part of the soundscape from Miltown Malbay to Melbourne.\n\nThe organising mistake to avoid is sorting them into rigid \"rhythm\" and \"melody\" columns. In Clare, the button accordion often leads the tune. Tenor banjo doubles fiddle melody an octave up while adding percussive attack. Guitar supplies harmony but may introduce counter-melody. Bodhrán marks pulse — yet overplaying turns it into a tempo dictator, the most common session faux pas. Concertina and accordion share bellows and free reeds but occupy different regional niches. Good musicianship means listening and role flexibility, not permanent labels.\n\nRead Traditional Voices I for fiddle, flute, whistle, pipes, and harp. Together, the two lessons map a typical pub ensemble without pretending every county sounds the same.\n\nThis lesson covers instruments that entered Irish sessions across different centuries — bodhrán, bones, guitar, bouzouki, mandolin, tenor banjo, concertina, button accordion, and harmonica. Some are indigenous or long-rooted (frame drum, bones); others arrived through diaspora, jazz age, or 1960s–70s fusion (guitar, bouzouki, banjo in trad lineup). All are now part of the soundscape from Miltown Malbay to Melbourne. The organising mistake to avoid is sorting them into rigid \"rhythm\" and \"melody\" columns. In Clare, the button accordion often leads the tune. Tenor banjo doubles fiddle melody an octave up while adding percussive attack. Guitar supplies harmony but may introduce counter-melody. Bodhrán marks pulse — yet overplaying turns it into a tempo dictator, the most common session faux pas. Concertina…",
