    "body": "The bodhrán (pronounced roughly BO-rawn) is a single-headed frame drum, typically 35–45 cm in diameter, with a wooden rim and goatskin or synthetic head. The player strikes with a tipper (cipín — a short beater, straight or curved) and alters tone by pressing the non-striking hand inside the skin against the rim. Wikipedia and ethnomusicologists debate whether the bodhrán is ancient or largely a nineteenth-century southwest Irish development; either way, its session prominence exploded in the twentieth-century revival, especially after ensemble experiments legitimised frame drum in trad bands.\n\nThe bodhrán (pronounced roughly BO-rawn) is a single-headed frame drum, typically 35–45 cm in diameter, with a wooden rim and goatskin or synthetic head. The player strikes with a tipper (cipín — a short beater, straight or curved) and alters tone by pressing the non-striking hand inside the skin against the rim. Wikipedia and ethnomusicologists debate whether the bodhrán is ancient or largely a nineteenth-century southwest Irish development; either way, its session prominence exploded in the twentieth-century revival, especially after ensemble experiments legitimised frame drum in trad bands.",
