    "body": "The player usually sits, drum held vertically on the knee or angled inward. Tipper technique varies: Kerry style may use rapid wrist strokes; West Limerick players favour smoother ride patterns; some use double-ended tippers for roll effects. The inside hand is the secret: pressing toward the rim raises pitch and dampens overtones; a light touch leaves the skin boomy; a firm press yields dry, tight clicks. Good bodhrán playing mirrors melody phrasing — accenting reel lift on beats two and four, jig pulse in groups of three — rather than metronomic thumping.\n\nOrnament on bodhrán means timbral variation: sliding the inside hand while striking, rim shots, or pausing during tune transitions. Less is often more.\n\nThe player usually sits, drum held vertically on the knee or angled inward. Tipper technique varies: Kerry style may use rapid wrist strokes; West Limerick players favour smoother ride patterns; some use double-ended tippers for roll effects. The inside hand is the secret: pressing toward the rim raises pitch and dampens overtones; a light touch leaves the skin boomy; a firm press yields dry, tight clicks. Good bodhrán playing mirrors melody phrasing — accenting reel lift on beats two and four, jig pulse in groups of three — rather than metronomic thumping.\n\nOrnament on bodhrán means timbral variation: sliding the inside hand while striking, rim shots, or pausing during tune transitions. Less is often more.",
