    "body": "Bodhrán supplies pulse and colour. It should enter after melody establishes tempo — experienced players listen before striking. In bands (The Chieftains, De Dannan, Lúnasa), bodhrán adds drive without replacing fiddle rhythm. In sessions, one bodhrán is a common etiquette rule; two uncordinated drums fight the tune.\n\nSeán Ó Riada (1931–1971) placed bodhrán in Ceoltóirí Chualann arrangements — controversial then, influential forever. Ó Riada treated Irish ensemble as orchestration, not accidental pub volume; bodhrán gained legitimacy beside harp, pipes, and fiddle on stage. Memorable detail: Before Ó Riada, some trad purists saw bodhrán as inferior or modern; after Ceoltóirí Chualann and The Chieftains, it became iconic.\n\nNotable players: Mel Mercier, Colm Murphy, John Joe Kelly (Lúnasa) — each with distinct touch (Mercier's Clare sensitivity, Kelly's athletic drive). Ronan Ó Snodaigh (Kíla) experiments with multicultural frame-drum vocabulary while rooted in Irish pulse — reminder that bodhrán technique continues to evolve beyond revival orthodoxy.\n\nBodhrán supplies pulse and colour. It should enter after melody establishes tempo — experienced players listen before striking. In bands (The Chieftains, De Dannan, Lúnasa), bodhrán adds drive without replacing fiddle rhythm. In sessions, one bodhrán is a common etiquette rule; two uncordinated drums fight the tune. Seán Ó Riada (1931–1971) placed bodhrán in Ceoltóirí Chualann arrangements — controversial then, influential forever. Ó Riada treated Irish ensemble as orchestration, not accidental pub volume; bodhrán gained legitimacy beside harp, pipes, and fiddle on stage. Memorable detail: Before Ó Riada, some trad purists saw bodhrán as inferior or modern; after Ceoltóirí Chualann and The Chieftains, it became iconic. Notable players: Mel Mercier, Colm Murphy, John Joe Kelly (Lúnasa) — each with distinct…",
