    "estimateMinutes": 4,
    "body": "If the instrument lessons taught you who speaks in an Irish session, this lesson teaches you what language they speak. Irish traditional music is organised around dance forms — reels, jigs, hornpipes, polkas, slides, and rarer metres — each with its own pulse, tempo range, and social history. Running alongside instrumental dance music is a parallel world of song: sean-nós in Irish, narrative ballads in English, drinking songs and laments, work songs and lilting. Ornamentation turns a skeleton tune into a recognisable regional accent; sets and variation turn a single melody into a living conversation.\n\nThis lesson is long because the subject is wide. Take it in sections: learn to identify the form first (reel versus jig is the classic beginner trap), then study ornament on your own instrument, then explore song traditions as separate repertoires that sometimes share melodies with dance tunes. By the end you should be able to foot-tap confidently, name what you are hearing, and understand why a Clare polka set and a Donegal highland feel like different countries wearing the same passport.\n\nIf the instrument lessons taught you who speaks in an Irish session, this lesson teaches you what language they speak. Irish traditional music is organised around dance forms — reels, jigs, hornpipes, polkas, slides, and rarer metres — each with its own pulse, tempo range, and social history. Running alongside instrumental dance music is a parallel world of song: sean-nós in Irish, narrative ballads in English, drinking songs and laments, work songs and lilting. Ornamentation turns a skeleton tune into a recognisable regional accent; sets and variation turn a single melody into a living conversation. This lesson is long because the subject is wide. Take it in sections: learn to identify the form first (reel versus jig is the classic…",
