    "estimateMinutes": 4,
    "body": "Before memorising tune names, train your ear for metre (how beats are grouped) and feel (how those beats are articulated). Written time signatures are guides, not prisons: hornpipes may be notated in 4/4 but played with a dotted swing; sean-nós song may float across bar lines; slow airs may have almost no fixed beat at all.\n\nA useful habit: tap your foot and say the pulse aloud. Reels want a steady ONE-two THREE-four drive. Double jigs want JIG-ged-y JIG-ged-y — two main beats per bar, each divided into three. If you tap reel rhythm to a jig, experienced players will notice immediately and the tune will feel wrong in your body even if every note is correct.\n\nFeel also includes lift — the subtle emphasis that makes dance music bounce. Lift comes from bowing (fiddle), tonguing (flute, whistle), bellows pressure (accordion, concertina), or rhythmic articulation (banjo). It is not the same as playing louder; it is playing with the dance impulse, even when no one is dancing.\n\nBefore memorising tune names, train your ear for metre (how beats are grouped) and feel (how those beats are articulated). Written time signatures are guides, not prisons: hornpipes may be notated in 4/4 but played with a dotted swing; sean-nós song may float across bar lines; slow airs may have almost no fixed beat at all. A useful habit: tap your foot and say the pulse aloud. Reels want a steady ONE-two THREE-four drive. Double jigs want JIG-ged-y JIG-ged-y — two main beats per bar, each divided into three. If you tap reel rhythm to a jig, experienced players will notice immediately and the tune will feel wrong in your body even if every note is correct. Feel also includes…",
