    "lessonSourceId": "regions-celtic-ireland-04-genres-forms",
    "estimateMinutes": 4,
    "body": "Reels are the workhorse of the Irish session. They are most often in 4/4 or notated in 2/2 (cut time) with a steady, driving pulse at moderate to fast tempo. A typical session might chain two or three reels before switching to jigs or hornpipes. Reels dominate because they suit the social energy of pub gatherings: forward motion, clear downbeats, room for melodic variation without losing the thread. Structurally, most reels are binary — an A part and a B part, each often eight bars, repeated. Musicians learn parts first, then sets of multiple tunes. Classic examples heard everywhere include The Wind That Shakes the Barley, The Maid Behind the Bar, Drowsy Maggie, The Silver Spear, and The Musical Priest. Diaspora fiddlers from Michael Coleman to Cape Breton players have spread reel repertoire so widely that the same tune may carry different ornaments in Sligo, Donegal, and New England. Reel bowing on fiddle often slurs across beats to create back-beat lift — a long bow stroke followed by a crisp catch. Flute and whistle players articulate with tonguing patterns that…\n\nReels are the workhorse of the Irish session. They are most often in 4/4 or notated in 2/2 (cut time) with a steady, driving pulse at moderate to fast tempo. A typical session might chain two or three reels before switching to jigs or hornpipes. Reels dominate because they suit the social energy of pub gatherings: forward motion, clear downbeats, room for melodic variation without losing the thread. Structurally, most reels are binary — an A part and a B part, each often eight bars, repeated. Musicians learn parts first, then sets of multiple tunes. Classic examples heard everywhere include The Wind That Shakes the Barley, The Maid Behind the Bar, Drowsy Maggie, The Silver Spear, and The Musical Priest.…",
