    "body": "Double jigs are in 6/8 time with a strong two-beat feel per bar: each beat divides into three eighth notes. They are the second most common session form after reels. Dance associations are broad — group céilí figures, solo step dance, and informal kitchen dancing all use jig rhythm.\n\nFamous double jigs include The Swallowtail Jig, The Irish Washerwoman (often played faster and lighter than newcomers expect), , Banish Misfortune, and The Lilting Banshee. The A and B parts repeat; musicians often pair a well-known jig with a lesser-known companion in a set.\n\nDouble jigs are in 6/8 time with a strong two-beat feel per bar: each beat divides into three eighth notes. They are the second most common session form after reels. Dance associations are broad — group céilí figures, solo step dance, and informal kitchen dancing all use jig rhythm.\n\nFamous double jigs include The Swallowtail Jig, The Irish Washerwoman (often played faster and lighter than newcomers expect), , Banish Misfortune, and The Lilting Banshee. The A and B parts repeat; musicians often pair a well-known jig with a lesser-known companion in a set.",
