    "estimateMinutes": 4,
    "body": "Single jigs share 6/8 metre but lighten the rhythmic pattern — more crotchet–quaver (quarter–eighth) motion, less of the continuous triplet stream of a double jig. They are less common in modern sessions than doubles but appear in regional repertoire and older publications. Listen for a slightly skipping quality compared with the rolling drive of a double jig. If you transcribe a single jig from a recording, you may be tempted to write endless triplet groups because that is how double jigs look on the page. Resist: single-jig phrasing often alternates longer notes with quick catches, and fiddlers may bow the pattern differently from flute players' tonguing. The Frost Is All Over and The Hag at the Churn are often played with single-jig feel in some regions — always verify against a local source rather than a generic tune book. Single jigs also appear in step-dance teaching as light jigs — the same metre family with a pedagogical name. Musicians backing beginners learn that \"jig\" in a dance class may mean single-jig articulation even when the session next door plays doubles…\n\nSingle jigs share 6/8 metre but lighten the rhythmic pattern — more crotchet–quaver (quarter–eighth) motion, less of the continuous triplet stream of a double jig. They are less common in modern sessions than doubles but appear in regional repertoire and older publications. Listen for a slightly skipping quality compared with the rolling drive of a double jig. If you transcribe a single jig from a recording, you may be tempted to write endless triplet groups because that is how double jigs look on the page. Resist: single-jig phrasing often alternates longer notes with quick catches, and fiddlers may bow the pattern differently from flute players' tonguing. The Frost Is All Over and The Hag at the Churn are often played…",
