    "body": "Slip jigs use 9/8 — three beats per bar, each divided into three eighth notes. They feel lighter and more asymmetrical than double jigs, with a graceful lilt often linked to soft-shoe step dance in the competitive tradition. Hardiman the Fiddler, The Butterfly, and The Foxhunter's Jig are slip-jig standards.\n\nSlip jigs reward patience: the nine-beat bar can feel like it \"turns\" in the middle if you are used to six-beat jigs. Dancers and musicians share vocabulary here — slip-jig steps match the tune's elongated phrase shapes.\n\nSlip jigs use 9/8 — three beats per bar, each divided into three eighth notes. They feel lighter and more asymmetrical than double jigs, with a graceful lilt often linked to soft-shoe step dance in the competitive tradition. Hardiman the Fiddler, The Butterfly, and The Foxhunter's Jig are slip-jig standards.\n\nSlip jigs reward patience: the nine-beat bar can feel like it \"turns\" in the middle if you are used to six-beat jigs. Dancers and musicians share vocabulary here — slip-jig steps match the tune's elongated phrase shapes.",
