    "body": "Hornpipes are usually notated in 4/4 but played with a dotted, long-short bounce — slower than reels, with a swagger sometimes linked to sailor tunes and stage hornpipe dances. Names like Off to California, The Sailor's Hornpipe, The Boys of Bluehill, and The Harvest Home signal the type immediately to experienced players.\n\nRegional dotting varies: Donegal fiddlers and Clare players may articulate hornpipes differently. Some sessions play hornpipes relatively straight; others lean heavily into the dotted snap. Compare recordings rather than trusting a single metronome marking.\n\nHornpipes suit hard-shoe step dance in competition — the dotted rhythm matches heel-and-toe percussion on the floor. Musicians accompanying feiseanna learn hornpipe tempo as a distinct category from session reels.\n\nHornpipes are usually notated in 4/4 but played with a dotted, long-short bounce — slower than reels, with a swagger sometimes linked to sailor tunes and stage hornpipe dances. Names like Off to California, The Sailor's Hornpipe, The Boys of Bluehill, and The Harvest Home signal the type immediately to experienced players.\n\nRegional dotting varies: Donegal fiddlers and Clare players may articulate hornpipes differently. Some sessions play hornpipes relatively straight; others lean heavily into the dotted snap. Compare recordings rather than trusting a single metronome marking.\n\nHornpipes suit hard-shoe step dance in competition — the dotted rhythm matches heel-and-toe percussion on the floor. Musicians accompanying feiseanna learn hornpipe tempo as a distinct category from session reels.",
