    "body": "Irish polkas are in 2/4 with a brisk, simple harmonic motion — not to be confused with Central European classical polkas or Oktoberfest clichés. They are strongest in Munster, especially Sliabh Luachra (the Cork–Kerry border region) and west Kerry. Polkas often appear in sets alternating with slides; dancers in set-dancing revival scenes depend on them.\n\nExamples include Denis Murphy's Polka, Johnny I Hardly Knew Ye (as instrumental polka sets), and numerous unnamed local polkas passed ear-to-ear. Accordion and fiddle carry the form; guitar and bouzouki outline chords in D, G, and A.\n\nPolka rhythm is uneven in a good way — a slight lilt on the off-beat that keeps feet moving in crowded kitchens. If you play polkas like stiff march tunes, set dancers will tell you with their eyes.\n\nIrish polkas are in 2/4 with a brisk, simple harmonic motion — not to be confused with Central European classical polkas or Oktoberfest clichés. They are strongest in Munster, especially Sliabh Luachra (the Cork–Kerry border region) and west Kerry. Polkas often appear in sets alternating with slides; dancers in set-dancing revival scenes depend on them. Examples include Denis Murphy's Polka, Johnny I Hardly Knew Ye (as instrumental polka sets), and numerous unnamed local polkas passed ear-to-ear. Accordion and fiddle carry the form; guitar and bouzouki outline chords in D, G, and A. Polka rhythm is uneven in a good way — a slight lilt on the off-beat that keeps feet moving in crowded kitchens. If you play polkas like stiff…",
