    "body": "Do not confuse slides (the 12/8 tune form) with slides (the ornament — a portamento between notes). The tune type is a jig family metre concentrated in Sliabh Luachra and west Kerry. Slides feel like jigs stretched and relaxed — twelve eighth notes grouped in a distinctive pulse (often described as four beats of three, but felt differently from 6/8 doubles).\n\nThe Star Above the Garter, The Gallant Boys of Tipperary (slide settings), and local Sliabh Luachra repertoire staples belong here. Slides are session gold in Kerry and can sound unfamiliar to players who learned only from generic Dublin tune books.\n\nMemorable detail: A musician who knows reels and double jigs but has never played a slide may still \"sort of\" play one — and miss the regional accent entirely. Learn at least one slide from a Sliabh Luachra recording.\n\nDo not confuse slides (the 12/8 tune form) with slides (the ornament — a portamento between notes). The tune type is a jig family metre concentrated in Sliabh Luachra and west Kerry. Slides feel like jigs stretched and relaxed — twelve eighth notes grouped in a distinctive pulse (often described as four beats of three, but felt differently from 6/8 doubles). The Star Above the Garter, The Gallant Boys of Tipperary (slide settings), and local Sliabh Luachra repertoire staples belong here. Slides are session gold in Kerry and can sound unfamiliar to players who learned only from generic Dublin tune books. Memorable detail: A musician who knows reels and double jigs but has never played a slide may still \"sort…",
