    "body": "Mazurkas appear in Irish trad as a minority form — often three beats per bar (3/4) with a characteristic accent pattern borrowed from Polish and continental dance music. They entered Irish repertoire through travelling musicians, sheet music, and cross-cultural exchange in the nineteenth century. You will not hear mazurkas every night in a Dublin session, but they appear in collections and in some regional players' tune bags.\n\nIrish mazurkas such as Sonny's Mazurka or settings of continental tunes carry a different lilt from waltzes: emphasis lands on beat two or three in ways waltz players may find surprising. Treat them as a formal cousin to polkas and slides — regional spice, not session default.\n\nMazurkas appear in Irish trad as a minority form — often three beats per bar (3/4) with a characteristic accent pattern borrowed from Polish and continental dance music. They entered Irish repertoire through travelling musicians, sheet music, and cross-cultural exchange in the nineteenth century. You will not hear mazurkas every night in a Dublin session, but they appear in collections and in some regional players' tune bags.\n\nIrish mazurkas such as Sonny's Mazurka or settings of continental tunes carry a different lilt from waltzes: emphasis lands on beat two or three in ways waltz players may find surprising. Treat them as a formal cousin to polkas and slides — regional spice, not session default.",
