    "body": "Drinking songs (The Wild Rover, Whiskey in the Jar, Finnegan's Wake) are communal, chorus-heavy, and designed for group participation. They are not musically subtle — they are social glue. Laments (She Moved Through the Fair, The Lament for Limerick, sean-nós equivalents in Irish) slow the room down. Air and phrasing matter more than rhythmic drive. Work songs — waulking songs, milling songs, sailor labour chants — survive in fragments and revival performances. They remind us trad is not only pub reels but labour rhythms tied to tasks. Sean-nós and lilting sometimes preserve work-song pulse without the original workplace. Scottish Gaelic waulking songs (òrain luaidh) influenced Irish song research; in Ireland, spailpín (migrant labourer) songs and fishing refrains carry similar call-and-response energy. A verse might be solo; the chorus returns with collective force — the same social architecture as pub ballads, but tied to work rather than leisure. For instrumentalists, work-song rhythm explains why some reels feel like they \"pull\" — the tune encodes a physical task's repetition. When you hear older singers talk about \"the time of the songs,\" they…\n\nDrinking songs (The Wild Rover, Whiskey in the Jar, Finnegan's Wake) are communal, chorus-heavy, and designed for group participation. They are not musically subtle — they are social glue. Laments (She Moved Through the Fair, The Lament for Limerick, sean-nós equivalents in Irish) slow the room down. Air and phrasing matter more than rhythmic drive. Work songs — waulking songs, milling songs, sailor labour chants — survive in fragments and revival performances. They remind us trad is not only pub reels but labour rhythms tied to tasks. Sean-nós and lilting sometimes preserve work-song pulse without the original workplace. Scottish Gaelic waulking songs (òrain luaidh) influenced Irish song research; in Ireland, spailpín (migrant labourer) songs and fishing refrains carry similar call-and-response…",
