    "body": "Irish trad culture is bilingual in practice even when musicians speak only English. Song titles, chorus lines, and session banter mix Irish and English. Competitions at Oireachtas na Gaeilge privilege Irish-language song; pub stages may be mostly English — same island, different rooms.\n\nPoetic forms in Irish song include structured verse types (some inherited from bardic poetry) and looser narrative verse in English ballads. Assonance, internal rhyme, and place naming anchor songs to locality — a listener from West Clare recognises different references than a Dubliner.\n\nCommunal singing shapes performance: choruses invite the room; verses may be solo. Story-telling function matters as much as melody — a song explains who we were, who we lost, and who we argue about still. Instrumental players who never learn lyrics miss half the emotional map.\n\nIrish trad culture is bilingual in practice even when musicians speak only English. Song titles, chorus lines, and session banter mix Irish and English. Competitions at Oireachtas na Gaeilge privilege Irish-language song; pub stages may be mostly English — same island, different rooms. Poetic forms in Irish song include structured verse types (some inherited from bardic poetry) and looser narrative verse in English ballads. Assonance, internal rhyme, and place naming anchor songs to locality — a listener from West Clare recognises different references than a Dubliner. Communal singing shapes performance: choruses invite the room; verses may be solo. Story-telling function matters as much as melody — a song explains who we were, who we lost, and who we argue about…",
