    "estimateMinutes": 4,
    "body": "Lilting (portaireacht) is wordless vocal music — the voice imitates fiddle, pipes, or accordion rhythm and melody using nonsense syllables (\"diddly-dee\", \"thrum-a-lum\", and endless local variants). It served house parties when no instrument was available, taught dance rhythm, and passed tunes between generations.\n\nLilting is not comedy — though it can be joyful. Skilled lilters like Percy French (also a songwriter) or competition lilters at fleadhanna demonstrate rhythmic precision. For learners, lilting is a pedagogy tool: if you can lilt a reel, you understand its pulse without finger technique blocking you.\n\nLilting connects to bones playing and dance masters who counted rhythm aloud — voice as percussion and melody together.\n\nLilting (portaireacht) is wordless vocal music — the voice imitates fiddle, pipes, or accordion rhythm and melody using nonsense syllables (\"diddly-dee\", \"thrum-a-lum\", and endless local variants). It served house parties when no instrument was available, taught dance rhythm, and passed tunes between generations.\n\nLilting is not comedy — though it can be joyful. Skilled lilters like Percy French (also a songwriter) or competition lilters at fleadhanna demonstrate rhythmic precision. For learners, lilting is a pedagogy tool: if you can lilt a reel, you understand its pulse without finger technique blocking you.\n\nLilting connects to bones playing and dance masters who counted rhythm aloud — voice as percussion and melody together.",
