    "estimateMinutes": 4,
    "body": "Slow airs are lyrical, often free-tempo melodies — frequently drawn from sean-nós song or lament tradition. They are not dance tunes. Putting a slow air unannounced in the middle of a reel set is a session faux pas unless the room expects it (commemoration, singer's turn, concert programme).\n\nAir performance favours sparse ornament, dynamic swells, and personal phrasing. Harp, fiddle, uilleann pipes, and flute each have air traditions. Danny Boy (lyric to Londonderry Air) is the famous export; Irish players also cherish The Coolin, Give Me Your Hand, and pipe airs like Tabhair dom do Lámh.\n\nAir rhythm breathes with the phrase — metronomes are poor teachers here. Listen to pipers and fiddlers who specialise in airs; copy where they pause, not only which notes they play.\n\nSlow airs are lyrical, often free-tempo melodies — frequently drawn from sean-nós song or lament tradition. They are not dance tunes. Putting a slow air unannounced in the middle of a reel set is a session faux pas unless the room expects it (commemoration, singer's turn, concert programme). Air performance favours sparse ornament, dynamic swells, and personal phrasing. Harp, fiddle, uilleann pipes, and flute each have air traditions. Danny Boy (lyric to Londonderry Air) is the famous export; Irish players also cherish The Coolin, Give Me Your Hand, and pipe airs like Tabhair dom do Lámh. Air rhythm breathes with the phrase — metronomes are poor teachers here. Listen to pipers and fiddlers who specialise in airs; copy where they…",
