    "body": "No genres lesson is complete without Francis O'Neill (1848–1936) — Chicago police chief, collector, and publisher of Music of Ireland (1903) and Dance Music of Ireland (1907), among others. O'Neill did not invent the tunes; he captured names, settings, and variants from Irish musicians in America and linked them to a notated tradition that beginners still use.\n\nPrinted collections standardise but also freeze — a tune in O'Neill may differ from a Clare session version by an entire part. Use books as indexes to find melodies, then go to recordings for ornament and tempo. The Irish Traditional Music Archive (ITMA) in Dublin holds manuscripts, 78 rpm discs, and field recordings that complicate any single printed version.\n\nMemorable detail: O'Neill's Chicago network included musicians who emigrated after the famine and the Land War — the same diaspora that spread reels to recording studios and sent Coleman-style playing back to Ireland.\n\nNo genres lesson is complete without Francis O'Neill (1848–1936) — Chicago police chief, collector, and publisher of Music of Ireland (1903) and Dance Music of Ireland (1907), among others. O'Neill did not invent the tunes; he captured names, settings, and variants from Irish musicians in America and linked them to a notated tradition that beginners still use. Printed collections standardise but also freeze — a tune in O'Neill may differ from a Clare session version by an entire part. Use books as indexes to find melodies, then go to recordings for ornament and tempo. The Irish Traditional Music Archive (ITMA) in Dublin holds manuscripts, 78 rpm discs, and field recordings that complicate any single printed version. Memorable detail: O'Neill's Chicago…",
