    "body": "Irish dance and Irish traditional music grew up together — not as separate art forms that later collaborated, but as a single social technology for marking time, building community, and carrying identity. A reel in the session is not abstract metre; it is the same pulse that once drove figures in a farmhouse kitchen and still drives sixteen people through The Siege of Ennis in a parish hall. A hornpipe's dotted bounce is the sound hard-shoe dancers hear in their heels.\n\nThis lesson maps dance traditions from the travelling masters of the seventeenth and eighteenth centuries to the global spectacle of Riverdance, from improvised sean-nós on a half-door to adjudicated feiseanna where tempo arguments ruin friendships. Musicians need this map. You cannot play well for dancers if you do not know which beat is strong, which tradition you are serving, and why a set dancer's polka is not a session reel with the same name.\n\nIrish dance and Irish traditional music grew up together — not as separate art forms that later collaborated, but as a single social technology for marking time, building community, and carrying identity. A reel in the session is not abstract metre; it is the same pulse that once drove figures in a farmhouse kitchen and still drives sixteen people through The Siege of Ennis in a parish hall. A hornpipe's dotted bounce is the sound hard-shoe dancers hear in their heels. This lesson maps dance traditions from the travelling masters of the seventeenth and eighteenth centuries to the global spectacle of Riverdance, from improvised sean-nós on a half-door to adjudicated feiseanna where tempo arguments ruin friendships. Musicians need this map.…",
