    "body": "By the early twentieth century, Gaelic League halls, national schools, and church halls replaced the itinerant master with certified teachers. An Coimisiún (1930) and later organisations standardised step syllabi. Set and céilí dancing were taught for national revival as well as fun — feet became evidence of cultural continuity.\n\nThe travelling master did not vanish so much as transform into the weekly class, the summer school instructor, and the YouTube channel — but the pedagogical insight remains: dance is learned in community, not only from a screen.\n\nMemorable detail: Some accounts claim dance masters taught on platforms or doors to protect floors and amplify sound — a direct ancestor of sean-nós dance on a half-door.\n\nBy the early twentieth century, Gaelic League halls, national schools, and church halls replaced the itinerant master with certified teachers. An Coimisiún (1930) and later organisations standardised step syllabi. Set and céilí dancing were taught for national revival as well as fun — feet became evidence of cultural continuity.\n\nThe travelling master did not vanish so much as transform into the weekly class, the summer school instructor, and the YouTube channel — but the pedagogical insight remains: dance is learned in community, not only from a screen.\n\nMemorable detail: Some accounts claim dance masters taught on platforms or doors to protect floors and amplify sound — a direct ancestor of sean-nós dance on a half-door.",
