    "lessonSourceId": "regions-celtic-ireland-05-dance",
    "estimateMinutes": 4,
    "body": "Dances such as The Siege of Ennis, The Walls of Limerick, The High Caul Cap, The Sweets of May, and The Fairy Reel appear in repertoires worldwide thanks to Comhaltas teaching networks and diaspora classes. Each dance has a prescribed figure sequence: advance and retire, house, swing, basket, lead around. Musicians must hold steady tempo through long figures; dancers depend on predictable eight-bar phrasing.\n\nThe Siege of Ennis — named for the Clare town — is the céilí dance many beginners learn first. Its figures teach basic skills used across the repertoire: facing partners, progressing around the set, and recovering when someone turns the wrong way (inevitable, human, forgiven).\n\nDances such as The Siege of Ennis, The Walls of Limerick, The High Caul Cap, The Sweets of May, and The Fairy Reel appear in repertoires worldwide thanks to Comhaltas teaching networks and diaspora classes. Each dance has a prescribed figure sequence: advance and retire, house, swing, basket, lead around. Musicians must hold steady tempo through long figures; dancers depend on predictable eight-bar phrasing.\n\nThe Siege of Ennis — named for the Clare town — is the céilí dance many beginners learn first. Its figures teach basic skills used across the repertoire: facing partners, progressing around the set, and recovering when someone turns the wrong way (inevitable, human, forgiven).",
