    "estimateMinutes": 4,
    "body": "Before parish halls and competition stages, house parties (cóisirí) were the default dance floor. Furniture pushed aside, musician in the corner, half-door lifted for sean-nós, neighbours arriving with bottles and stories. Set figures survived because four couples fit in a kitchen; céilí long lines needed bigger rooms.\n\nMusicians at house parties played longer on each tune — fewer dancers, more conversation between figures, slower social tempo than modern feis stages. Revival nostalgia sometimes romanticises poverty; still, the scale matters: a reel that works for two couples waltzing through a house swing may feel wrong when sixteen beginners collide in Siege of Ennis.\n\nIf you ever play in someone's kitchen, watch the low beam, protect the pipes from steam, and accept that tempo will be whatever the oldest dancer in the room needs. That is tradition too.\n\nBefore parish halls and competition stages, house parties (cóisirí) were the default dance floor. Furniture pushed aside, musician in the corner, half-door lifted for sean-nós, neighbours arriving with bottles and stories. Set figures survived because four couples fit in a kitchen; céilí long lines needed bigger rooms. Musicians at house parties played longer on each tune — fewer dancers, more conversation between figures, slower social tempo than modern feis stages. Revival nostalgia sometimes romanticises poverty; still, the scale matters: a reel that works for two couples waltzing through a house swing may feel wrong when sixteen beginners collide in Siege of Ennis. If you ever play in someone's kitchen, watch the low beam, protect the pipes from steam, and accept…",
