    "estimateMinutes": 4,
    "body": "Why does one fiddle tune have four names, three keys, and two conflicting stories about who wrote it? Because Irish traditional music survived colonisation, famine, emigration, and language shift through oral transmission — and every link in that chain left fingerprints. A hearth version became a dancing master's version; a harper's air became a collector's manuscript; a Sligo fiddler's New York 78 became the \"correct\" way to play the tune back in Ireland.\n\nThis lesson follows the music as far back as the sources allow: medieval annals and hymns, harp patronage and its collapse, travelling fiddlers and pipers, print collectors and police chiefs, diaspora shellac and radio revival, Comhaltas competitions and summer schools, archives and UNESCO — ending with the debate every living tradition faces: competition clarity versus session swing, video access versus regional accent, innovation versus source.\n\nYou have met tune types, instruments, and dance forms in earlier tiers. Here you learn how the repertoire moved — and why understanding transmission makes you a better musician, not only a better historian.\n\nWhy does one fiddle tune have four names, three keys, and two conflicting stories about who wrote it? Because Irish traditional music survived colonisation, famine, emigration, and language shift through oral transmission — and every link in that chain left fingerprints. A hearth version became a dancing master's version; a harper's air became a collector's manuscript; a Sligo fiddler's New York 78 became the \"correct\" way to play the tune back in Ireland. This lesson follows the music as far back as the sources allow: medieval annals and hymns, harp patronage and its collapse, travelling fiddlers and pipers, print collectors and police chiefs, diaspora shellac and radio revival, Comhaltas competitions and summer schools, archives and UNESCO — ending with the…",
