    "estimateMinutes": 4,
    "body": "Medieval and early modern Irish poetry often blurred verse and music. Surviving airs are scarce before 1700 — Wikipedia's Irish music collecting article notes how little composed Irish music from that era survives — but literary texts and later transcriptions preserve a song world that fed the slow-air and sean-nós streams. Rop tú mo baile (Be Thou My Vision) — a sixth-century hymn attributed to Dallán Forgaill — was published in English in 1905 by Mary Elizabeth Byrne and is widely sung today in churches and folk settings. It exemplifies how early Irish sacred verse re-enters modern repertoire through scholarly translation and arrangement. The Blackbird of Belfast Lough (Int én bec / An t-éan beag) — a medieval Irish lyric — has been translated by poets including Seamus Heaney, Ciaran Carson, and Frank O'Connor, and recorded in modern interpretations (e.g. Pádraigín Ní Uallacháin's Songs of the Scribe, choral work by Anúna, projects by Caitríona O'Leary). Such pieces connect \"ancient Ireland\" to living performance — not as museum pieces but as material contemporary artists re-voice. Collectors from the eighteenth century onward…\n\nMedieval and early modern Irish poetry often blurred verse and music. Surviving airs are scarce before 1700 — Wikipedia's Irish music collecting article notes how little composed Irish music from that era survives — but literary texts and later transcriptions preserve a song world that fed the slow-air and sean-nós streams. Rop tú mo baile (Be Thou My Vision) — a sixth-century hymn attributed to Dallán Forgaill — was published in English in 1905 by Mary Elizabeth Byrne and is widely sung today in churches and folk settings. It exemplifies how early Irish sacred verse re-enters modern repertoire through scholarly translation and arrangement. The Blackbird of Belfast Lough (Int én bec / An t-éan beag) — a medieval Irish lyric…",
