    "body": "Seán Ó Riada (1931–1971), born John Reidy in Cork, became the single most influential figure in the mid-twentieth-century revival. As music director at Radio Éireann (from 1954) and the Abbey Theatre (1955–1962), he programmed traditional music for national audiences. His film scores Mise Éire (1959) and Saoirse? (1960) paired orchestra with Irish melody; his mass in Irish is still sung in Gaeltacht churches.\n\nIn 1961 he founded Ceoltóirí Chualann, an ensemble that arranged trad for concert presentation without abandoning source material — legitimising stage formats that purists had suspected. His work inspired Planxty, The Bothy Band, Clannad, and later Altan, Dervish, Lúnasa, and others.\n\nThe Chieftains grew directly from that environment — taking similar repertoire to international concert halls and recording with global collaborators. The Clancy Brothers and Tommy Makem, The Dubliners, Ryan's Fancy, Sweeney's Men, and Tom Lenihan (folklore collector-performer) fed the 1960s folk boom. Wikipedia notes a second wave after the lull: trad was not continuous peak popularity but cyclical renewal.\n\nSeán Ó Riada (1931–1971), born John Reidy in Cork, became the single most influential figure in the mid-twentieth-century revival. As music director at Radio Éireann (from 1954) and the Abbey Theatre (1955–1962), he programmed traditional music for national audiences. His film scores Mise Éire (1959) and Saoirse? (1960) paired orchestra with Irish melody; his mass in Irish is still sung in Gaeltacht churches. In 1961 he founded Ceoltóirí Chualann, an ensemble that arranged trad for concert presentation without abandoning source material — legitimising stage formats that purists had suspected. His work inspired Planxty, The Bothy Band, Clannad, and later Altan, Dervish, Lúnasa, and others. The Chieftains grew directly from that environment — taking similar repertoire to international concert halls and…",
