    "estimateMinutes": 4,
    "body": "Scoil Samhraidh Willie Clancy (Willie Clancy Summer School) in Miltown Malbay, County Clare, has run since 1973, honouring the uilleann piper Willie Clancy (1918–1973). Each July thousands attend classes in pipes, fiddle, flute, concertina, dance, and song — daytime tuition, recitals, set dancing, and all-night sessions. The school sits in the heart of Clare's tourist-trad corridor; critics worry about overcrowding; devotees call it pilgrimage.\n\nClancy learned from the blind piper Garrett Barry (1847–1899), who walked west Clare playing house dances — a direct teacher line from famine-era piping to UNESCO recognition. Noel Hill, the concertina master from Lissycasey, listened extensively to Clancy, Paddy Canny, and neighbours who learned at house dances — another transmission chain from kitchen to world stage.\n\nOther summer schools — Scoil Éigse, Queen Maeve International Summer School in Sligo (fiddle, flute, sean-nós), regional camps — repeat the model: concentrated teaching, social dancing, and session culture in one week.\n\nScoil Samhraidh Willie Clancy (Willie Clancy Summer School) in Miltown Malbay, County Clare, has run since 1973, honouring the uilleann piper Willie Clancy (1918–1973). Each July thousands attend classes in pipes, fiddle, flute, concertina, dance, and song — daytime tuition, recitals, set dancing, and all-night sessions. The school sits in the heart of Clare's tourist-trad corridor; critics worry about overcrowding; devotees call it pilgrimage. Clancy learned from the blind piper Garrett Barry (1847–1899), who walked west Clare playing house dances — a direct teacher line from famine-era piping to UNESCO recognition. Noel Hill, the concertina master from Lissycasey, listened extensively to Clancy, Paddy Canny, and neighbours who learned at house dances — another transmission chain from kitchen to world stage.…",
