    "body": "The Irish Traditional Music Archive (ITMA) — Taisce Cheol Dúchais Éireann — co-founded in 1987 by Nicholas Carolan and Harry Bradshaw, is the national reference archive for traditional song, instrumental music, and dance. Built on Brendan Breathnach's collections and expanded through donations from RTÉ, libraries, and private collectors, ITMA holds thousands of sound recordings, books, photographs, sheet music, and DVDs. Carolan directed for twenty-eight years; Grace Toland succeeded him in 2015. Publications include editions of the James Goodman manuscripts and catalogues of Bunting holdings.\n\nNa Píobairí Uilleann (NPU) promotes uilleann piping, pipe making, and teaching. When uilleann piping joined UNESCO's Representative List of the Intangible Cultural Heritage of Humanity in 2017 (reference 01264), the inscription highlighted makers and teachers — not only stage soloists. Piping is bellows-blown, with chanter, drones, and regulators for chordal accompaniment; the craft faces ongoing need for apprentices in reed-making and bag-leather work.\n\nThe Irish Traditional Music Archive (ITMA) — Taisce Cheol Dúchais Éireann — co-founded in 1987 by Nicholas Carolan and Harry Bradshaw, is the national reference archive for traditional song, instrumental music, and dance. Built on Brendan Breathnach's collections and expanded through donations from RTÉ, libraries, and private collectors, ITMA holds thousands of sound recordings, books, photographs, sheet music, and DVDs. Carolan directed for twenty-eight years; Grace Toland succeeded him in 2015. Publications include editions of the James Goodman manuscripts and catalogues of Bunting holdings. Na Píobairí Uilleann (NPU) promotes uilleann piping, pipe making, and teaching. When uilleann piping joined UNESCO's Representative List of the Intangible Cultural Heritage of Humanity in 2017 (reference 01264), the inscription highlighted makers and teachers —…",
