    "estimateMinutes": 4,
    "body": "George Petrie (1790–1866) collected melodies when oral tradition still outpaced print; his work fed antiquarian interest in \"ancient Irish airs\" at a time when nationalism sought cultural proof. Brendan Breathnach (1912–1985) systematised dance-tune collection in Ceol Rince na hÉireann, volumes that became backbone material for ITMA. Nicholas Carolan, ITMA's founding director, edited facsimiles of Neale (1724) and championed the James Goodman manuscripts from Trinity College Dublin — hundreds of pre-famine melodies.\n\nArchives do not end oral tradition; they anchor it. When a dispute arises over whether a tune is \"really\" a slide or a single jig, manuscripts and field recordings offer evidence — not verdicts. Performers still decide.\n\nGeorge Petrie (1790–1866) collected melodies when oral tradition still outpaced print; his work fed antiquarian interest in \"ancient Irish airs\" at a time when nationalism sought cultural proof. Brendan Breathnach (1912–1985) systematised dance-tune collection in Ceol Rince na hÉireann, volumes that became backbone material for ITMA. Nicholas Carolan, ITMA's founding director, edited facsimiles of Neale (1724) and championed the James Goodman manuscripts from Trinity College Dublin — hundreds of pre-famine melodies.\n\nArchives do not end oral tradition; they anchor it. When a dispute arises over whether a tune is \"really\" a slide or a single jig, manuscripts and field recordings offer evidence — not verdicts. Performers still decide.",
