    "estimateMinutes": 4,
    "body": "James Morrison (3 May 1893–1947) from Drumfin, Riverstown, County Sligo, was employed by the Gaelic League at seventeen to teach Connacht-style step dancing. He emigrated to New York in 1915, won the New York Feis fiddle in 1918, and recorded alongside Coleman and Killoran. Morrison taught hundreds of Irish-American students fiddle, flute, banjo, and button accordion — his nickname \"The Professor\" reflects pedagogy as much as performance. His recordings imported Sligo repertoire so powerfully that, in many areas, local fiddle styles yielded to Coleman-Morrison models for decades. Listening to Morrison beside Coleman reveals shared Sligo roots and individual differences in bow pressure and reel selection.\n\nJames Morrison (3 May 1893–1947) from Drumfin, Riverstown, County Sligo, was employed by the Gaelic League at seventeen to teach Connacht-style step dancing. He emigrated to New York in 1915, won the New York Feis fiddle in 1918, and recorded alongside Coleman and Killoran. Morrison taught hundreds of Irish-American students fiddle, flute, banjo, and button accordion — his nickname \"The Professor\" reflects pedagogy as much as performance. His recordings imported Sligo repertoire so powerfully that, in many areas, local fiddle styles yielded to Coleman-Morrison models for decades. Listening to Morrison beside Coleman reveals shared Sligo roots and individual differences in bow pressure and reel selection.",
